>
  <p>
    <em>"Why the number 141?"</em> Is the first question most people have. The truth
    is, it’s a long and rather meandering story. I don’t think you need every detail,
    suffice to say that it’s been my personal number, whatever that means, since I
    was 15 years old and the video game <em>Call of Duty Modern Warfare 2</em> was
    released. It’s part of my email address, it’s something I’ve had engraved on pocket
    knives and wallets. One time in a classroom, when the teacher asked us what our
    favorite numbers were, I shot my hand up and declared “141!”, and the whole class
    seemed to think it was funny for some reason.
  </p>

  <p>
    So really it’s a personal thing. I like the symmetry of it, I like how it
    sounds. But it wasn’t until fairly recently that I noticed something else:
    that the numbers “one-four-one” sound a lot like the phrase <strong
      >One for one.</strong
    >
  </p>

  <p>And that, to me, means reciprocity.</p>

  <p>
    We all have a comparative advantage, we all have something that makes us
    special. A gift to give, if you will. If we hone that gift, and we figure
    out how to use it to make other peoples’ lives better, I believe that’s when
    we come closest to fulfilling whatever purpose we were put here to do.
  </p>

  <p>
    My gifts are writing and design. My craft is website development. I believe
    in taking the time to do things right, and in the endless pursuit of
    mastery. I would rather take a little more time and make something amazing
    than cut corners for convenience’s sake. I’ve spent the last seven years
    learning what makes websites great, and how to build them from scratch. Yes
    there’s a financial transaction involved, but if I can make something just
    for you that’s excellent and that makes your life better in some way, and
    you and I form a connection, then that’s where the real wealth lies. I
    enrich you life, and you enrich mine.
  </p>
  <p>One for one.</p>
  <p>-Alex</p>
</Layout>
